1
00:00:06,753 --> 00:00:10,548
In 2018, Olympic National Park
had a goat problem.

2
00:00:10,965 --> 00:00:13,509
Mountain goats, which were
invasive to the area,

3
00:00:13,509 --> 00:00:16,763
were charging at visitors
and trampling rare plants.

4
00:00:17,096 --> 00:00:20,433
They were after one thing: salt.

5
00:00:21,225 --> 00:00:24,771
As hikers worked up a sweat or relieved
themselves along trails,

6
00:00:24,771 --> 00:00:29,358
they created an irresistible draw
for these salt-hungry animals.

7
00:00:29,734 --> 00:00:34,781
The situation became so dangerous that
crews had to capture hundreds of goats

8
00:00:34,781 --> 00:00:36,824
and have them airlifted out of the park.

9
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:39,994
These goats’ behavior may
seem exceptional,

10
00:00:39,994 --> 00:00:43,539
but all animals rely on salt,
or sodium chloride,

11
00:00:43,539 --> 00:00:46,084
for many basic biological functions.

12
00:00:46,375 --> 00:00:48,294
An influx of sodium, for example,

13
00:00:48,294 --> 00:00:52,673
is the trigger that causes nerve cells
to fire and relay messages.

14
00:00:52,757 --> 00:00:57,845
While chloride is a key component
in stomach acid that helps digest food.

15
00:00:58,137 --> 00:01:01,432
And humans, just like
those salt-crazed goats,

16
00:01:01,432 --> 00:01:05,937
have gone to similarly great lengths
to feed their own salt fixes,

17
00:01:06,437 --> 00:01:10,441
Our hunter-gatherer ancestors
satisfied the need for salt

18
00:01:10,441 --> 00:01:13,277
primarily by eating other animals.

19
00:01:13,486 --> 00:01:16,739
Plants also contain salt,
but far less.

20
00:01:16,906 --> 00:01:20,409
So, when agriculture flourished
around 11,000 years ago,

21
00:01:20,409 --> 00:01:23,830
the practice of adding salt to food began.

22
00:01:24,330 --> 00:01:28,543
Seaside civilizations filled shallow
basins with seawater

23
00:01:28,543 --> 00:01:30,378
to evaporate in the sun,

24
00:01:30,378 --> 00:01:32,421
leaving salt crystals behind.

25
00:01:32,755 --> 00:01:36,801
Inland, ancient settlers along
a salty river in central China

26
00:01:36,884 --> 00:01:42,223
boiled river water in vats to evaporate
the water and harvest the salt crystals.

27
00:01:42,557 --> 00:01:46,394
Meanwhile, miners in what's
today Hallstatt, Austria,

28
00:01:46,394 --> 00:01:49,105
cut into mountains to harvest rock salt,

29
00:01:49,105 --> 00:01:52,692
long buried in the earth
after ancient seas evaporated.

30
00:01:53,568 --> 00:01:57,780
Salt wasn't just a biological necessity,
but an essential tool,

31
00:01:57,905 --> 00:02:02,410
allowing ancient civilizations to preserve
fish, meats, produce, and dairy.

32
00:02:03,119 --> 00:02:06,455
Salt kills the bacteria
that would otherwise spoil food

33
00:02:06,455 --> 00:02:09,500
by sucking water from
these microbe’s cells.

34
00:02:09,876 --> 00:02:13,254
And salt was also prized
for making food taste better.

35
00:02:13,588 --> 00:02:18,968
A pinch will mask bitter notes and bring
out sour, sweet, and umami flavors.

36
00:02:19,302 --> 00:02:22,972
A heavier pour will mask
sweetness and up the umami.

37
00:02:23,556 --> 00:02:28,728
However, exactly how salt performs
these transformations is still a mystery.

38
00:02:29,061 --> 00:02:31,522
So wherever salt was plentiful,

39
00:02:31,647 --> 00:02:35,568
humans harvested it
to trade with salt-hungry regions.

40
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:38,487
As early as the 1st millennia CE,

41
00:02:38,613 --> 00:02:45,119
huge caravans of camels moved slabs of
salt mined in the Sahara to West Africa.

42
00:02:45,661 --> 00:02:48,080
Here, salt supplies were so rare

43
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:53,419
that people handed over equal quantities
of gold dust for the precious commodity.

44
00:02:54,128 --> 00:02:56,923
In ancient Rome, the mineral was so valued

45
00:02:56,964 --> 00:03:01,469
that it’s believed that soldiers
received part of their pay in salt—

46
00:03:01,636 --> 00:03:05,431
which may be why the word salary
contains “sal,”

47
00:03:05,723 --> 00:03:07,725
the Latin root for salt.

48
00:03:08,434 --> 00:03:12,939
As for salt on tables, the first known
containers for dispensing salt,

49
00:03:12,939 --> 00:03:16,442
called salt cellars,
appeared in ancient Greece.

50
00:03:17,026 --> 00:03:20,488
Many scholars believe that at banquets
in medieval Europe,

51
00:03:20,488 --> 00:03:23,407
these elaborate cellars marked
social status,

52
00:03:23,532 --> 00:03:28,746
as the most important society members
sat on one end of the table above the salt

53
00:03:28,871 --> 00:03:31,374
and the less important sat below it.

54
00:03:32,208 --> 00:03:37,380
But over the years, unequal access
to salt didn’t go uncontested.

55
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:41,217
In the 18th century,
an unpopular salt tax in France,

56
00:03:41,217 --> 00:03:45,930
which nobility was exempt from paying,
helped spur the French Revolution.

57
00:03:46,639 --> 00:03:50,643
In 1930, Gandhi’s salt march
in protest of British laws

58
00:03:50,643 --> 00:03:54,981
that forbade Indians from collecting
or selling salt on their own land,

59
00:03:55,147 --> 00:03:58,192
helped launch the Indian
Independence Movement.

60
00:03:58,818 --> 00:04:00,361
By the turn of the 20th century,

61
00:04:00,361 --> 00:04:04,198
with industrialization and the discovery
of additional salt deposits,

62
00:04:04,198 --> 00:04:08,661
global salt production exploded
and the price dropped.

63
00:04:09,078 --> 00:04:12,748
Soon, salt producers were harvesting
millions of metric tons

64
00:04:12,748 --> 00:04:14,458
of salt every year,

65
00:04:14,583 --> 00:04:18,754
using the same methods that have
been honed for thousands of years,

66
00:04:18,754 --> 00:04:22,758
only with the help of more
sophisticated and explosive tools.

67
00:04:23,092 --> 00:04:27,847
And sprinkling this abundance of salt
on every meal was easier than ever before

68
00:04:28,097 --> 00:04:31,600
thanks to the Morton Salt Company,
who in 1911,

69
00:04:31,851 --> 00:04:33,853
began adding magnesium carbonate

70
00:04:33,853 --> 00:04:36,689
to prevent salt from clumping
in humid kitchens.

71
00:04:37,106 --> 00:04:38,691
With salt free to flow,

72
00:04:38,691 --> 00:04:41,902
the invention of salt and pepper
shakers followed.

73
00:04:42,194 --> 00:04:44,905
The pair became seasoning soulmates,

74
00:04:45,072 --> 00:04:49,410
regularly adorning tabletops across
North America and Europe.

75
00:04:50,161 --> 00:04:54,957
So how did pepper come to join salt
on so many dinner tables?

